import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Target repos — ports stay in host, modules live in ecosystem repositories — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Target repos — ports stay in `host`, modules live in ecosystem repositories — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0128"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0128-target-repos.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0128-target-repos.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/design/0128-target-repos.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0128-target-repos-—-ports-stay-in-host-modules-live-in-ecosystem-repositories-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">ADR-0128: Target repos — ports stay in <code>host</code>, modules live in ecosystem repositories — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0128-target-repos-—-ports-stay-in-host-modules-live-in-ecosystem-repositories-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0128: Target repos — ports stay in host, modules live in ecosystem repositories — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0128 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the mechanism, tables and rationale.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The <code>handl</code> repository is a single Cargo workspace of <strong>90 members</strong> — engine substrate, ports, adapters, hosts, and vendored language packages — with 209 third-party crates in the resolve graph and 129 integration test binaries. A full nextest run is ~71 minutes on this 11 GB host; <code>target/</code> is 2.9 GB.</p><p>The motivation for splitting is fourfold and measured, not aesthetic:</p><ul><li><strong>Heavy optional deps in everyone&#39;s build.</strong> <code>handl-kit-canonical</code> hard-depends on <code>handl-plugin-engine-exec-cranelift</code> (its <em>only</em> normal dependency — <code>handl-runtime</code>, <code>handl-kit-data-canonical</code> and <code>handl-defining-handl</code> reference it only in <code>[dev-dependencies]</code>). Every consumer of the canonical kit — the LSP host, <code>plugin-compile-js</code>, any parse-only embedder — therefore compiles <strong>14 cranelift crates</strong>.</li><li><strong>Implementation detail in platform wiring.</strong> <code>plugin-ext-sdkgen</code> is 486 lines of which <strong>205 are Python-specific</strong> (<code>PythonSpec</code>, <code>load_python_spec</code>, <code>generate_python</code>, <code>build_python_hnl</code>, <code>build_code_assignment</code>) — a target&#39;s internals living inside the dispatcher.</li><li><strong>Ownership / contributors.</strong> No boundary exists between &quot;platform&quot; and &quot;integration&quot; that an outside contributor could be scoped to.</li><li><strong>CI time.</strong> One workspace = one graph; a change anywhere rebuilds everywhere.</li></ul><p>The seams are already clean <em>at the crate level</em>. The graph is dense — only 9 leaves, <code>handl-platform</code> has 57 inbound deps, <code>handl-language</code> 49 — but the <em>port</em> layer is real: <code>plugin-sdkgen</code> declares <code>SdkgenBackend</code>/<code>Emitter</code>/<code>DocSource</code>, <code>plugin-compile</code> declares <code>Compiler</code>/<code>CompilerRegistry</code>, <code>plugin-lib-kv</code> declares <code>KeyValueStore</code>, <code>plugin-ext-kit</code> declares <code>LibraryRegistry</code>.</p><p>What blocks independence is <strong>three static registration tables</strong>:</p><ol><li><code>plugin-ext-sdkgen/src/lib.rs</code> — <code>if kind != &quot;c&quot; &amp;&amp; kind != &quot;webidl&quot; &amp;&amp; kind != &quot;python&quot;</code> plus a ~90-line <code>match</code> arm per target.</li><li><code>plugin-ext-compile/src/dispatcher.rs</code> — <code>registry.register(Box::new( JsCompiler)); registry.register(Box::new(LlvmCompiler::new()));</code></li><li><code>plugin-ext-kit/src/library_registry.rs</code> — <code>reg.register(&quot;otlp-std&quot;, || Box::new(...))</code>, <code>kv-hashmap</code>, …</li></ol><p>…and <code>host-cli/Cargo.toml</code> statically depends on <code>plugin-compile-js</code> / <code>plugin-compile-llvm</code>.</p><p>The <code>.hnl</code> side has its own constraint: <strong>68 of 95 packages are referenced from <code>crates/</code></strong> and 31 of those are <code>include_str!</code>-pinned to <code>plugin-lib-stdlib</code>&#39;s <code>ModuleSpec</code> table (<code>hnl: &amp;&#39;static str</code>), which makes them compile-time welded to the stdlib crate.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Repo = target/ecosystem; module = one implementation of one port; a module registers one or more named targets.</strong> Target name ≠ repo name.</p><p><strong>Module directories carry the specific name, never the generalized port name.</strong> The convention is <code>modules/&lt;port&gt;-&lt;specific&gt;</code>: the port is a <em>namespace prefix</em>, not a rename. <code>web/modules/sdkgen-webidl</code> registers the <code>webidl</code> target — and when <code>web/</code> later grows a second sdkgen target (dts, cssom, …) it lands beside it as <code>sdkgen-&lt;that&gt;</code> without colliding. Likewise <code>native/modules/exec-cranelift</code> / <code>native/modules/compile-llvm</code> keep their dep&#39;s name, and <code>dot/</code>/<code>term/</code>/<code>sexp/</code> are three separate repos — repo-per-target consistency for compiler targets; each holds <code>modules/compile-&lt;target&gt;</code> so the dir name stands alone.</p><p>Platform keeps: ports, core (<code>handl-platform</code>, <code>handl-language</code>, <code>handl-value</code>, <code>handl-runtime</code>), the generic kit machinery (<code>handl-kit-data</code> — kit/fragment <em>types</em>, consumed by <code>plugin-ext-kit</code> which stays) and <code>handl-kit-arrow-lower</code> (the <code>Ast → Term</code> lowerer — <code>handl-runtime</code> links it, and <code>lang/</code> must not create a platform↔lang cycle), engines (<code>plugin-engine*</code>), wiring (<code>plugin-ext-*</code> — minus the static tables and target internals), loaders/appliers, <code>host-tooling</code> (the shared host contract both host repos depend on), and <strong>the three <code>plugin-lib-*</code> crates that are machinery, not content</strong>: <code>plugin-lib-handl</code> (the generic <code>.hnl</code> module loader — <code>HandlModule</code>/<code>OpRegistry</code>; 19 inbound deps, including every moving lib), <code>plugin-lib-tag</code> (tagged-values host primitive used by the typecheckers), <code>plugin-lib-scheduler</code> (test-engine machinery). Every other <code>plugin-lib-*</code> moves to <code>stdlib/</code>.</p><h3 id="extraction-list" tabindex="-1">Extraction list <a class="header-anchor" href="#extraction-list" aria-label="Permalink to “Extraction list”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>what</th><th>→ repo / module</th><th>port</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-sdkgen-backend-c</code></td><td><code>c/modules/sdkgen-c</code></td><td><code>SdkgenBackend</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-sdkgen-backend-webidl</code></td><td><code>web/modules/sdkgen-webidl</code></td><td><code>SdkgenBackend</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-sdkgen-emitter-python</code> + the 205 Python lines of <code>plugin-ext-sdkgen</code></td><td><code>python/modules/sdkgen-python</code></td><td><code>Emitter</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-js</code></td><td><code>web/modules/compile-js</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-llvm</code></td><td><code>native/modules/compile-llvm</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-engine-exec-cranelift</code></td><td><code>native/modules/exec-cranelift</code></td><td>exec engine</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-dot</code></td><td><code>dot/modules/compile-dot</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-term</code></td><td><code>term/modules/compile-term</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-sexp</code></td><td><code>sexp/modules/compile-sexp</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-handl</code></td><td><code>handl/modules/compile-handl</code></td><td><code>Compiler</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-otlp</code>, <code>plugin-lib-otlp-std</code></td><td><code>otlp/modules/{otlp,otlp-std}</code></td><td><code>LibraryPlugin</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-kv-hashmap</code></td><td><code>stdlib/modules/kv-hashmap</code></td><td><code>KeyValueStore</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-stdlib</code> + its 31 <code>include_str!</code>ed packages</td><td><code>stdlib/crates/plugin-lib-stdlib</code> + <code>stdlib/packages/</code></td><td>module chain</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-kv</code></td><td><code>stdlib/modules/kv</code></td><td>kv port — its only consumer is <code>kv-hashmap</code>, so the port rides to stdlib</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-{crypto,encoding,parse,serialize,char-native}</code></td><td><code>stdlib/modules/{crypto,encoding,parse,serialize,char-native}</code></td><td>library impls</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-{lex,parse,eval,typecheck,compile,run-compiled}</code> (6)</td><td><code>stdlib/modules/lib-lang-*</code></td><td>language toolchain modules</td></tr><tr><td><code>.hnl</code> packages pinned to the moving libs — <code>crypto</code>, <code>encoding</code>, <code>parse</code>, <code>serialize</code>, <code>kv</code>, <code>kv_hashmap</code>, <code>lang_*</code>, <code>char</code>/<code>char-native</code></td><td><code>stdlib/packages/</code></td><td>(moves with crate)</td></tr><tr><td><code>.hnl</code> packages <code>otlp</code>, <code>otlp_std</code></td><td><code>otlp/</code></td><td>(moves with crate)</td></tr><tr><td><code>.hnl</code> web block (<code>js</code>, <code>fetch-js</code>, <code>ws_frame{,-handl}</code>, <code>ws-c</code>, <code>ws-whatwg-c</code>, <code>ws_whatwg_wasm</code>, <code>http_rfc</code>, <code>serve{,-epoll}</code>, <code>concurrent-epoll</code>)</td><td><code>web/</code></td><td>(not pinned)</td></tr><tr><td><code>generated/*-webidl-browser</code> (13 dirs)</td><td><code>web/</code></td><td>(not pinned)</td></tr><tr><td><code>host-cli</code> + <code>plugin-ext-cli</code></td><td><code>cli/</code></td><td>the <code>hnl</code> distribution — its dep list <em>is</em> its target set</td></tr><tr><td><code>host-lsp</code> + <code>plugin-lsp</code> + <code>plugin-lsp-std</code> + <code>plugin-ext-lsp</code></td><td><code>lsp/</code></td><td>the LSP server distribution</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-ffi</code></td><td><code>ffi/modules/ffi</code></td><td>FFI marshalling — sole normal consumer is <code>kit-canonical</code>; pulls <code>cranelift</code> + <code>libloading</code>, the last heavy deps in platform</td></tr><tr><td><code>handl-kit-data-canonical</code> + canonical <code>data/*.yaml</code> + <code>handl-packages/</code> remainder</td><td><code>lang/</code></td><td>Handl-the-language as data (ADR-0044 made literal)</td></tr><tr><td><code>kit-canonical</code></td><td><code>kit-canonical/crates/kit-canonical</code></td><td>the canonical <em>kit assembly</em> — wires language data + stdlib + engines into a <code>Kit</code>; consumed by hosts</td></tr><tr><td><code>handl-defining-handl</code>, <code>plugin-engine-typecheck-nbe</code></td><td><code>meta/crates/</code></td><td>self-hosting/NbE experiments — zero-consumer leaves</td></tr><tr><td><code>docs/</code> (VitePress site) + the sibling <code>handl-lang/docs/</code> (top-level ADRs, outside the platform repo)</td><td><code>docs/</code></td><td>documentation hub; each repo&#39;s API reference is generated from its workspace tree and published into <code>docs/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>plugin-compile-handl</code> moves to <strong><code>handl/</code></strong> — the repo for Handl as a target — under strict target-per-repo consistency. <strong>Naming:</strong> the platform repo (today <code>handl-lang/handl</code>) is renamed <strong>host/</strong>, since &quot;platform&quot; is exactly what it now holds; <code>handl/</code> then means the <em>language target</em>, not the workspace. <code>host-tooling</code> stays — it is the shared host contract consumed by <em>both</em> <code>cli/</code> and <code>lsp/</code>; sole-consumer satellites ride to their host&#39;s repo (<code>plugin-ext-cli</code> → <code>cli/</code>; <code>plugin-lsp</code>, <code>plugin-lsp-std</code>, <code>plugin-ext-lsp</code> → <code>lsp/</code>).</p><p><strong>Hosts sit at the top of the fan.</strong> <code>host-cli</code>&#39;s 74-crate closure and <code>host-lsp</code>&#39;s 48 become explicit git-dep lists in their repos: the <code>hnl</code> binary&#39;s target set is exactly what <code>cli/</code> declares. This also makes the registries load-bearing — the backends are no longer in-tree to fall back on.</p><h3 id="registration-link-time-first" tabindex="-1">Registration: link-time first <a class="header-anchor" href="#registration-link-time-first" aria-label="Permalink to “Registration: link-time first”">​</a></h3><p>Phase one keeps the ports&#39; existing <code>register()</code> surfaces but drives them from a <strong>feature-gated target registry</strong> in <code>plugin-sdkgen</code> / <code>plugin-compile</code> / <code>plugin-ext-kit</code>: the host opts targets in via Cargo features on its own manifest, each feature enabling a repo module. The <code>plugin-loader-native</code> dylib path is the documented future for true zero-repackage plugin delivery.</p><p>A consequence in the sdkgen namespace: a <em>target</em> composes a backend and an emitter, which may come from different repos — <code>hnl sdkgen python</code> is backend <code>c</code> + emitter <code>python</code>. The registry key is the target record <code>(backend_id, emitter_id)</code>, so <code>python/</code> depends on <code>c/</code>&#39;s module at resolution time, not at the crate level.</p><h3 id="hnl-ownership-rule" tabindex="-1"><code>.hnl</code> ownership rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#hnl-ownership-rule" aria-label="Permalink to “.hnl ownership rule”">​</a></h3><p>One <code>include_str!</code> per package, in the crate that owns it; every other consumer takes a <code>pub const</code> re-export. <code>hello</code>, <code>transform</code> and <code>stats</code> are today pinned by two or more crates — they get a single owner when <code>stdlib/</code> moves. <code>handl-packages/</code> itself <strong>splits</strong>: packages pinned by staying crates stay in platform; the 31 stdlib-pinned ones move with <code>plugin-lib-stdlib</code>; unpinned ecosystem packages move with their repo.</p><h3 id="resolution" tabindex="-1">Resolution <a class="header-anchor" href="#resolution" aria-label="Permalink to “Resolution”">​</a></h3><p>Git deps + <code>[patch]</code> for local development — <strong>local only, no publishing</strong> for now:</p><div class="language-toml"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">handl-plugin-sdkgen </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> {</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> git </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">https://github.com/handl-lang/host</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">,</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> tag </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">v0.1.0</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> }</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">[</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">patch</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">.</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">&quot;https://github</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">.</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">com/handl-lang/host&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">handl-plugin-sdkgen </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> {</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> path </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">../host/crates/plugin-sdkgen</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> }</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>[patch]</code> is local-dev only; CI resolves the tag. A <code>dev-link</code> script generates the patch block across sibling checkouts. Manifests gain <code>license</code>/<code>description</code>/<code>repository</code> now so future publishing is a no-op.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Good.</strong> Each repo builds only its own graph; <code>cranelift</code> and <code>clang-sys</code>/<code>inkwell</code> leave the default build; <code>plugin-ext-sdkgen</code> loses 205 foreign lines; ownership boundaries exist; the C-FFI <code>.hnl</code> block unblocks because the pins move <em>with</em> <code>plugin-lib-stdlib</code> — no module-chain seam needed for those.</p><p><strong>Costs.</strong> A change to a port (<code>Compiler</code>, <code>SdkgenBackend</code>) becomes a multi-PR atomic change plus a git rev bump — mitigated by <code>tag</code>-pinning, not eliminated. <code>kit-canonical</code> gains a git dependency on <code>stdlib/</code> — a deliberate inversion: the platform now depends on the stdlib package, not vice versa. Shared <code>.hnl</code> sources pinned by multiple crates need the <code>pub const</code> re-export rule before any move.</p><p><strong>Private-repo auth.</strong> Every repo is private except <strong><code>docs/</code></strong> (the published hub). <code>platform/</code> was public during the transition and <code>cli/</code> was created public; both are private now. Git deps bypass registries entirely — <code>cargo</code> just clones — so consumers need <code>CARGO_NET_GIT_FETCH_WITH_CLI=1</code> (or <code>net.git-fetch-with-cli</code> in <code>.cargo/config.toml</code>) for ssh auth, and each repo&#39;s CI needs a read credential for its sibling deps. Cheap today; flip to public when the &quot;external contributor&quot; motivation becomes real.</p><p><strong>The <code>lang/</code> cycle.</strong> <code>plugin-engine-test</code>, <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>, <code>plugin-ext-typecheck</code> and <code>host-tooling</code> have <em>normal</em> deps on <code>kit-canonical</code> — and <code>kit-canonical</code> depends on platform engines. A naive move creates a cross-repo cycle. The seam that breaks it is the same one ADR-0099 established: those consumers take <code>&amp;Language</code>/a kit handle as a parameter and the concrete canonical kit is assembled in the <code>kit-canonical/</code> repo (which <code>cli/</code>/<code>lsp/</code> git-depend on alongside <code>lang/</code>). This injection is Phase-0 work, not a <code>lang/</code>-phase surprise.</p><p><strong>The <code>0.8</code> seam, concretely</strong> (measured against the real call sites, 2026-10-15):</p><ul><li><strong><code>Plugin: Send + Sync</code> is the binding constraint.</strong> A <code>Kit</code> holds <code>RefCell</code>s and is <code>!Sync</code>, so no plugin struct may hold one — today or ever. Injection therefore cannot be &quot;store the kit&quot;; it is: <ul><li>a <strong><code>&amp;Language</code>/<code>Arc&lt;Language&gt;</code></strong> where the consumer only reads the language definition (<code>Language: Send + Sync</code> — proven by the existing <code>static LANG: OnceLock&lt;Language&gt;</code> in <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>), or</li><li>an <strong><code>Arc&lt;dyn Fn(...) -&gt; ... + Send + Sync&gt;</code> capability closure</strong> where the consumer needs kit <em>behavior</em> (e.g. <code>kit.typecheck_handl_with_prelude</code>, <code>kit.handl_lint_adapter().resolver()</code>, <code>kit.lex_with_spans</code>).</li><li>The host keeps today&#39;s per-thread memoization: it owns a <code>thread_local! RefCell&lt;Option&lt;Kit&gt;&gt;</code> and injects a <em>capture-less</em> closure (<code>|src, p| KIT.with(|k| …)</code>) — capture-free, so the closure is <code>Send + Sync</code> while the kit stays thread-local. The plugin becomes a pure adapter with no kit types in its signature.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>plugin-engine-test</code> needs a factory, not a value.</strong> Scheduler workers each build their own <code>Runtime</code> on-thread (<code>thread_local!</code>, because <code>Rc&lt;dyn ModuleLookup&gt;</code> cannot cross threads), so the runner must receive something it can invoke <em>inside</em> the job closure: <code>Arc&lt;dyn Fn() -&gt; Result&lt;Runtime, String&gt; + Send + Sync&gt;</code>. <code>HandlModule</code> and <code>with_loader_disabled</code> already live in <code>plugin-lib-handl</code> (the kit merely re-exports them) — those imports just change paths.</li><li><strong><code>host-tooling</code> is the worst current shape:</strong> <code>BasicSemanticTokens:: run</code> assembles <code>Kit::new()</code> <strong>per LSP request</strong>. The plugin must hold the injected lex capability from construction; <code>SpannedToken</code>/ <code>TokenClasses</code> are kit-defined types and either move to a lower crate or the injection is closure-shaped over them.</li><li><strong><code>dev-dependencies</code> on <code>kit-canonical</code> are legal and stay.</strong> Cargo permits cyclic dev-deps, and these four crates&#39; tests genuinely want the canonical kit. Only <em>normal</em> deps create the platform↔lang cycle; the work is severing exactly those four.</li><li><strong>Hosts keep their normal dep.</strong> <code>host-cli</code>/<code>host-lsp</code> move to <code>cli/</code>/<code>lsp/</code> anyway, where a git dep on <code>lang/</code> is the intended direction — they are the assembly point that injects.</li><li><strong>Precedent already exists:</strong> <code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>&#39;s <code>*_with_language(lang, src)</code> variants and <code>lint_source_with_language</code>/<code>lint_source_with_prelude_and_language</code> are the same seam in miniature. The 0.8 change extends that convention to the whole surface rather than inventing a new one.</li></ul><p><strong>Deferred.</strong> <code>plugin-loader-native</code> dylib registration (true zero-repackage); <code>c/</code>&#39;s 6 generated <code>*-c-linux-x86_64/</code> dirs and the remaining <code>-c</code> package block pending item-7 scope; <code>graphql/</code>/<code>pg/</code>/ <code>sqlite/</code> as greenfield repos once a first module exists.</p><p><strong>Landed — the <code>FragmentSource::DataFile</code> seam.</strong> <code>handl-kit-data</code> now reads a serialized <code>Language</code> from a data file (<code>FragmentSource::DataFile</code>) and loads a kit manifest from a file or inline text (<code>DataKitManifest::load</code> / <code>load_from_text</code>); the parser is chosen by extension (JSON / YAML / TOML) and failures name the file. A module repo can now ship fragment data and kit manifests as <em>data</em> rather than <code>include_str!</code>-pinning them.</p><p><strong>Landed — the <code>.hnl</code> name-resolution seam.</strong> <code>.hnl</code> packages now import each other <em>by package name</em> through the <code>.handl.toml</code> <code>[dependencies]</code> table instead of <code>../</code> filesystem paths: <code>from &quot;ws-c&quot; import …</code> and <code>from &quot;libc/socket&quot; import …</code> resolve the first path segment through the manifest&#39;s name → package-dir map (<code>ModuleLoader::resolve_path</code> / <code>resolve_spec_to_file</code> already consulted it; the blind spots were the <em>declaration</em> paths — <code>hnl run</code>/<code>typecheck</code>/<code>lint</code>&#39;s import stripping and splice-name seeding in <code>host-cli</code>, and <code>plugin-engine-test</code>&#39;s <code>import_preamble</code> — which now discover the manifest and resolve dep specs themselves). <code>host-cli</code>&#39;s tooling kit is project-aware (<code>Kit::discover</code> on the CWD) so <code>hnl test</code> wires the deps into the module loader too. Coverage: <code>crates/handl-runtime/tests/dependency_import.rs</code> (dep-name specs, bare-name → <code>&lt;name&gt;.hnl</code>, shadowing precedence, in-dep relative imports) alongside <code>bare_module_import.rs</code> (module-chain lowering). A consuming repo declares the dep once in its <code>.handl.toml</code> — the repo boundary is a manifest dep, not a <code>../</code> path — and in-repo relative imports are unchanged.</p>`,38)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};